DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ffsscckk is used to check and optionally repair one or more Linux
       filesystems. _f_i_l_e_s_y_s_t_e_m can be a device name (e.g., _/_d_e_v_/_h_d_c_1,
       _/_d_e_v_/_s_d_b_2), a mount point (e.g., _/, _/_u_s_r, _/_h_o_m_e), or an filesystem
       label or UUID specifier (e.g.,
       UUID=8868abf6-88c5-4a83-98b8-bfc24057f7bd or LABEL=root). Normally, the
       ffsscckk program will try to handle filesystems on different physical disk
       drives in parallel to reduce the total amount of time needed to check
       all of them.

       If no filesystems are specified on the command line, and the --AA option
       is not specified, ffsscckk will default to checking filesystems in
       _/_e_t_c_/_f_s_t_a_b serially. This is equivalent to the --AAss options.

       The exit status returned by ffsscckk is the sum of the following
       conditions:

       00
           No errors

       11
           Filesystem errors corrected

       22
           System should be rebooted

       44
           Filesystem errors left uncorrected

       88
           Operational error

       1166
           Usage or syntax error

       3322
           Checking canceled by user request

       112288
           Shared-library error

       The exit status returned when multiple filesystems are checked is the
       bit-wise OR of the exit statuses for each filesystem that is checked.

       In actuality, ffsscckk is simply a front-end for the various filesystem
       checkers (ffsscckk._f_s_t_y_p_e) available under Linux. The filesystem-specific
       checker is searched for in the PPAATTHH environment variable. If the PPAATTHH
       is undefined then fallback to _/_s_b_i_n.

       Please see the filesystem-specific checker manual pages for further
       details.

